{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"halfdivine-emersed-tick","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A per-pass model configuration seam (one env var cannot express four passes)\n\n## Problem\n\nrebar's gates deliberately run different model classes per pass. On the direct-Anthropic path this\nworks today and is MEASURED: a real `rebar review-plan` made 41 calls on `anthropic:claude-opus-4-8`\n(Pass-1, which escalates up from the YAML `model_ladder`) and 1 on `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`\n(the Pass-2/Pass-4 verifier downgrade). The operator requirement is exactly that shape: Pass-1 on\nopus for most criteria, Pass-2/Pass-4 on sonnet.\n\nThere is no way to express that on any non-default provider, because the whole selection collapses\nonto ONE knob:\n\n- **The verifier downgrade inverts.** `review_kernel/verify.py:269` is\n  `verifier_default if model == default_model else (model or verifier_default)`, with\n  `default_model = \"claude-opus-4-8\"`. ANY Bedrock id is a non-default value, read as an explicit\n  operator choice, so the sonnet downgrade STOPS APPLYING and Pass-2/Pass-4 follow Pass-1 onto\n  whatever single model `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` names.\n- **Pass-1's ladder routes to the wrong provider.** `plan-review.yaml:62-65` sets\n  `model_ladder: [claude-haiku-4-5, claude-sonnet-4-6, claude-opus-4-8]` as BARE names, and\n  `config.py:333-341` maps the prefix `claude` to provider `anthropic`. So during a Bedrock run the\n  Pass-1 finders still resolve to DIRECT ANTHROPIC — a split-brain where the pass doing most of the\n  work never reaches the provider under test.\n- **Code review has no split at all.** `workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` declares no `model:` and no\n  `model_ladder:` on any step, so base/round_a/round_b (Pass-1), verify (Pass-2) and coach (Pass-4)\n  all take `cfg.model`. It never calls `resolve_verifier_model`.\n\n## Why this blocks two stories\n\n- **eb6e (production cutover)** scopes itself to \"the bot service's `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`\" — one env var\n  for four passes. Both single-model outcomes are wrong: opus-everywhere hard-fails Pass-2, which\n  pins `temperature=0` (MEASURED: `us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8` returns 400 `temperature is\n  deprecated for this model`; see [rebar:1903-868c-b704-4a80]), and sonnet-everywhere silently\n  downgrades Pass-1 with no test to catch it.\n- **0d76 (parity bar)** compares v1=Anthropic against v2=Bedrock. Without per-pass control the arms\n  differ in TWO variables (provider AND per-pass model class), so any criterion failure is\n  unattributable and any pass is unrepresentative of production. A full corpus run is ~3,200 model\n  calls; spending that on an unattributable comparison is the expensive way to learn this.\n\n## Approach (candidate, not a decision)\n\nMake the per-pass model resolvable per provider rather than inferred from one string. Sketch:\nexpress the mapping (Pass-1 finder ladder, Pass-2/Pass-4 verifier) as configuration keyed by\nprovider, so a Bedrock run names Bedrock inference-profile ids for each pass instead of inheriting\nbare `claude-*` names. Note `bedrock_model_id` is NOT the answer — a single scalar cannot express a\nmapping, which is part of why it was deleted [rebar:281f-3336-c668-4979].\n\nWhatever shape is chosen must also fix the `verify.py:269` equality test, which silently disables\nthe downgrade for every non-default model rather than only for a deliberate operator override.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a test asserts the RESOLVED model for each pass under a Bedrock configuration is the intended\n      class (opus-family for Pass-1, sonnet-family for Pass-2/Pass-4)\n- [ ] a test asserts no bare `claude-*` model id is resolved to provider `anthropic` during a run\n      configured for another provider\n- [ ] a test asserts the Pass-2/Pass-4 downgrade still applies when the operator has NOT explicitly\n      chosen a model, on a non-default provider\n- [ ] the code-review gate expresses its per-pass models explicitly, rather than taking `cfg.model`\n      for all four passes\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a real gate run on the non-default provider shows the per-pass model ids\n      actually used, cited from the run's usage log in a comment","id":"3768-789c-11ed-48be","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Per-pass model configuration seam: one env var cannot express four passes"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785459987634854001,"uuid":"ea2667b0-fd92-4df7-ae68-6b00b424f84f"}